#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091929 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "bcd31bde-a3c1-5805-8427-a7ff5f676525" :question/math-academy-id "e-3464" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\cos{x}+\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\cos x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{3π}{4}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nlies inside the specified domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/914/Source/Images/03de6ea8bbd2a7d0c85d8e4354af305d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "57d85629-a48b-5238-adbc-22a00ad3ba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-3465" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $\\cos x =1$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the solutions to $\\cos x = 1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\cos x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (1) = 0^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $0^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx = n (360^\\circ)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSince the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nis one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x = 0^\\circ$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091931 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "2e673146-b5b9-5cb7-bef5-d9eb129a16b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3466" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\cos x +\\sqrt 2 =0$ for $-180^\\circ< x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\cos{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = 135^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $135^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/914/Source/Images/f27bbc3e50265f3a6cb038a7557b1f26.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 180^{∘} + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$ from $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= X - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘} - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&=-135^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx =-135^\\circ, 135^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "54b6d02c-dd57-5898-adc8-b3203ff03717" :question/math-academy-id "e-3517" :question/problem "Calculate, in radians to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\cos x = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{4}{5}) = 0.643501… \\approx 0.644\n$$\n\nSince $0.644$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.644\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.644\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.644\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/914/Source/Images/aeff38a2f68bbd826a3d9dd86b92cb7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - 0.644 \\\\\n&= 5.639\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.6, 5.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f034b9c-70ae-51d9-87e2-69bb1f7e2537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265426} {:answer/id #uuid "90128f64-4e01-55e7-8563-6e1f2010bdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265427} {:answer/id #uuid "98513b92-5dfe-5ad3-b2fb-3a68dc71cf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265428} {:answer/id #uuid "830b4ba7-fec4-5ed9-8124-2c7b764d098d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265429} {:answer/id #uuid "09b5fce7-0be6-5ccb-a14c-0d462241873a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "830b4ba7-fec4-5ed9-8124-2c7b764d098d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265429} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ea14a7-8aed-5635-b543-4a9f8e75932a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c83a3b-43f7-5f39-a6a6-5d1a9c67c2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120887" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $60\\cdot 300=18\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd9d3da5-1997-5bf3-b3b5-16391da60a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265433} {:answer/id #uuid "5d669ecf-8ecd-5d2e-b938-90cc3edd9d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265434} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad4ba8b-800f-5955-ba4a-207118545c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265435} {:answer/id #uuid "23a6a3ae-7e93-542d-84bb-64d8fc67838b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265436} {:answer/id #uuid "db2b665d-83a5-5f2f-9fe1-51ffb34fa5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db2b665d-83a5-5f2f-9fe1-51ffb34fa5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265437} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33c0a9b-c9dd-5c35-bc30-e81cc341ab7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265432}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b6ef5ea-e806-5bce-9fb5-8b166565989c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120981" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f6d6e3-0bd3-5882-a560-864cf3b9d86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265440} {:answer/id #uuid "da7cb799-d005-5b30-8db5-96a733a4eb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265441} {:answer/id #uuid "79be831e-5882-5d90-a89e-9c1c35d3dfce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265442} {:answer/id #uuid "02b77fdb-e76e-5852-8333-e57cdd897182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265443} {:answer/id #uuid "8db63621-d061-5a45-bad1-c640de376009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db63621-d061-5a45-bad1-c640de376009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265444} :answer-field/id #uuid "e657490f-35bc-5404-8f3a-2bde413c1805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7de16d9d-6113-58f0-8744-682ffcd73ecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-120997" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5edc613-dbdb-574b-9113-c1ed74ef05a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265447} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c4fe88-429a-5f6f-a66e-38eee7fb13c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265448} {:answer/id #uuid "3acba09f-4895-5fb2-aca8-c2caa913c81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265449} {:answer/id #uuid "6effe6d6-ab8a-52de-b425-4f09e0201e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265450} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed46ffa-d115-598a-9f6c-a726a7bcbeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed46ffa-d115-598a-9f6c-a726a7bcbeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265451} :answer-field/id #uuid "14997c84-1ae3-554b-8b46-bf2170865922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1b641d9-36cf-5d43-ae0b-f1ea1e2a31f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-121001" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1d64d19-11a7-57fe-aa72-e44a8bbdbf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265454} {:answer/id #uuid "e7076f62-2b3c-5fd5-875d-2ca2b064c12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265455} {:answer/id #uuid "11694741-3022-5b74-883c-be55f5c9a4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265456} {:answer/id #uuid "6dae9ee0-804a-5964-a66c-81b53b665115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265457} {:answer/id #uuid "4f560dfe-f352-5811-a23e-22b9d94a26b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11694741-3022-5b74-883c-be55f5c9a4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265456} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62952e8-cf53-52cf-afce-e56797d09219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f2a7b85-3ffa-58fb-9d53-158260676a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121084" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5}=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =5-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=0$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b4922f-efb3-57ca-a6cb-bd84971a6382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265461} {:answer/id #uuid "46683718-66a2-556a-b62b-15e6122811cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={0}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265462} {:answer/id #uuid "784cc398-0b44-5d37-ba95-30aac8871b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265463} {:answer/id #uuid "b58d3212-f558-53e3-b1a2-34b714ebdabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265464} {:answer/id #uuid "0b67ab78-ce97-5c92-963a-439249374587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={0}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "784cc398-0b44-5d37-ba95-30aac8871b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265463} :answer-field/id #uuid "12281a27-ce6e-5e5f-a075-9abec95e86f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "834a030a-e5b9-51bb-9d9c-9c0ebbe3f880" :question/math-academy-id "q-121088" :question/problem "Solve the equation $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx={360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={0}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f3aa1c-e063-5520-b9e6-591e43992127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265468} {:answer/id #uuid "98ff9ecf-7229-55af-8d76-47ef6d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265469} {:answer/id #uuid "94696305-6792-588e-947c-b3eafa081628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265470} {:answer/id #uuid "78b19398-ae88-5f50-9bb9-c6ccdcb712a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265471} {:answer/id #uuid "704e88fe-1d04-54dc-b82f-ddb3e0d931fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "704e88fe-1d04-54dc-b82f-ddb3e0d931fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265472} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7fc347-a900-5431-abdf-f04ded18e1ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265467}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79f1b09d-5a24-562d-94a3-62292789098e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20214" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/6bc5aed39e6421b8ff2416edb75dc888c8f38ae14f99b3d6e28d8e4c9302908f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1203d39-3084-5f7f-95b7-75d57f5a894e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc49d79-847d-5d37-9051-c6978fa32004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1203d39-3084-5f7f-95b7-75d57f5a894e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf7035-46e4-5145-b79c-926c16bff8f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d228bc-6220-557f-b5e7-96edb3fbf836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28d228bc-6220-557f-b5e7-96edb3fbf836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265478} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cce5ad-08d7-5419-80e8-a8a4985f8621" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "efec1dda-577f-5294-a47d-8a3cd24247de" :question/math-academy-id "q-274588" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{8})=(112.024\\,3…)^{∘}≈{112.024}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${112.024}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={112}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/18b8161029f2013671daadc367e54e4f02d1d7fac2134ea4953a725236cbddac.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{67.976}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{112.024}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{112}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-112}^{∘}$ and $x={112}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bae55fd-66c3-5627-ad93-3c45dba8ae9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bae55fd-66c3-5627-ad93-3c45dba8ae9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a41e80-16b3-5ec5-8c63-ff0680d1c53d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a878f681-8c62-5b05-9d86-60ebea245b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a878f681-8c62-5b05-9d86-60ebea245b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e55b6d-4d5a-5c49-811d-0c593663ca9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c3f6ee4-25da-5eee-9c39-e29f0b38f7d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-338230" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d88379fcad6ee1bfc7f6c2159c814f92ca330dbacee191ebebb027bab306fe2c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c49a68-6813-51a8-8e23-1791f48f907a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265486} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc7fb0a-26d6-5e24-a12b-7ba19c893cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dc7fb0a-26d6-5e24-a12b-7ba19c893cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265487} :answer-field/id #uuid "382877e9-1903-58fc-8257-1becd41b96b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf4b1a0-f7cb-5ca0-9a72-a5fcc0d1e5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf4b1a0-f7cb-5ca0-9a72-a5fcc0d1e5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265489} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c052b6-cd19-5785-b38c-c553f3e78e67" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28eb9d47-7311-5c47-9e0a-827d0b6e01d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-338252" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde5d558-ddf1-557b-a66c-c1b577381475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265492} {:answer/id #uuid "837173a3-804c-5ff0-93f2-69b9076f5d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde5d558-ddf1-557b-a66c-c1b577381475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265492} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c61f7af-be24-567e-b818-738d931aeb7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c175c27d-4593-54ac-9856-1f5ed5d66330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c175c27d-4593-54ac-9856-1f5ed5d66330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec6c6079-9291-5277-9128-14ed401ec4ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265494}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e1b0798-16fd-5a51-a661-6bc33d230919" :question/math-academy-id "q-338258" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/144ed33e7739fbaf17e3f81dd9ae2fd0a2d8e4d622ced7cb5dd60c8d177b6c01.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={225}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e1e48f-5de9-5688-9c3b-b18c9a6b6d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e1e48f-5de9-5688-9c3b-b18c9a6b6d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265498} :answer-field/id #uuid "2581334b-3434-5278-9cb9-7c38868112e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265497} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe6052cd-40ad-5cf7-a220-fe376a972c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6052cd-40ad-5cf7-a220-fe376a972c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265500} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6b764b-200b-50d8-b36a-bb7c31ead8be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90d8ea8b-4e0a-538b-bdbc-28f62f92d15a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338261" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4 & =1 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca89b78a-382a-5f6f-a6ba-b7ea32a330b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca89b78a-382a-5f6f-a6ba-b7ea32a330b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265503} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60be9e3-b9dd-50a3-9b00-4276c0c419be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265502} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60127a9c-4f23-5299-b7cf-e508635eb41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60127a9c-4f23-5299-b7cf-e508635eb41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265505} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed5b4d4c-6794-5117-819a-a103c6236dfa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30d61a94-a432-5495-b43d-b92c98c3391b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338342" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{7})=1.281\\,044…≈1.2810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.2810$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=1.3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/1b1b50c4d13ebae81a4c8af45ec2c9fde8dc6810da78bd21f74ebcdd117a375e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.2810 \\\\  & ≈5.0022 \\\\  & ≈5.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=1.3$ and $x=5.0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c03d072e-c84b-5934-85cd-1d03b1a2f39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265508} {:answer/id #uuid "b564c853-cd0c-5876-9953-e57252d54a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b564c853-cd0c-5876-9953-e57252d54a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265509} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe01312-4008-520e-9dbf-93cd685440bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8a2d91-6ab5-5263-ae40-3d6822cb5de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e8a2d91-6ab5-5263-ae40-3d6822cb5de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265511} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0a8909-a153-5887-9a3e-5917acdaca55" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b6b30aa-3838-5b8c-8682-eaa8579ef511" :question/math-academy-id "q-338346" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{7})=2.013\\,707…≈2.0137\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.0137$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=2.0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.0137≈1.1279.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.1279.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/157096afa9d3de44710dfa11c99f1f699815156fbcd5d649c1b4048dbbba9913.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.1279 \\\\  & ≈4.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2.0$ and $x=4.3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755174b4-8da1-5917-81b3-079d52e3f4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "755174b4-8da1-5917-81b3-079d52e3f4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265514} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f34b66-0d44-5e56-bd44-8dc079a1dc71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19186045-6879-52ee-a9d6-a4cdb3e2e78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19186045-6879-52ee-a9d6-a4cdb3e2e78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265516} :answer-field/id #uuid "f756d634-f2e1-574c-8ff5-f47475f51b94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265515}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f33d23f5-c525-57bc-85e7-c27a008eaf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-338422" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{4}{9})=2.031\\,350…≈2.031\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.031$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.031≈1.111.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.111.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d416faad39ff978c5ad59b91ceee311d358818ee7bf486f0dea95b9ae6319978.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.111 \\\\  & ≈4.253 \\\\  & ≈4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff3020a-4098-5421-b2bf-bf2fc390c8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dff3020a-4098-5421-b2bf-bf2fc390c8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265519} :answer-field/id #uuid "855f1173-5dce-5b3f-a46a-5228eb8bd733" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115e897e-49a3-549b-8afc-b35062c87eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "115e897e-49a3-549b-8afc-b35062c87eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265521} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee68c436-f0cf-54a6-9342-7644485d45dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265520}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22b48a48-cf0d-5b45-afdb-643b245ba807" :question/math-academy-id "q-338688" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8ea1a5ca6b0dc0bd7867ab841c629f9966f2b97de4585b9c595a33af206c4a7f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4923efdd-f65c-5aee-9ea6-c386d4f9856e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4923efdd-f65c-5aee-9ea6-c386d4f9856e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265524} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c06654-cb97-5bb4-9ae4-62881b7893e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265523} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5adb5fa4-e92b-5241-8a37-c99a6451b6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5adb5fa4-e92b-5241-8a37-c99a6451b6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265526} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d55449-66ed-5796-abc8-c8c6dcd2ba2d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92118efb-6092-5861-938f-35cfcc896fb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338738" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find all solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3ade93d-a2f2-5836-abfc-384acec5ff8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265529} {:answer/id #uuid "da7731db-8d85-57ff-92cb-ab6a7ea0ffb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7731db-8d85-57ff-92cb-ab6a7ea0ffb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265530} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bfbe3b8-005d-5366-ab11-e52f9f0ddf60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265528}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67443d1b-c390-5dbe-9010-f3276669865e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338744" :question/problem "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=2\\pi $ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f083f61-fe2f-517e-9056-2ae59d95d4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265533} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1b4a88-404d-564c-941f-15cf03a7c73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265534} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2f0d92-5024-564b-ac78-c223c04dc862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265535} {:answer/id #uuid "111a4836-4164-50f9-a468-9835454d177e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265536} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6ba047-7eb6-5624-b3da-6fc8295594c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "111a4836-4164-50f9-a468-9835454d177e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265536} :answer-field/id #uuid "22abdfd4-164a-5e27-bbfa-11c150aab333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265532}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aed35d75-442a-5745-81f9-db85fcbf8be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72840" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=1.318\\,116…≈1.318\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.318$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.318.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8797bd2b1558e094b72d891fd344b356e5d579e4549b788bdd0507de23f171c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.318 \\\\  & =4.965\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.318\\cdot 4.965≈7\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43703d42-0970-5c9a-a5ca-d96c83bb26bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265540} {:answer/id #uuid "f35daf13-716b-58d4-98b9-88fca216f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265541} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ade304-0307-52af-890d-37adafa8a04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265542} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc4900d-2a15-5533-bc48-6fc1b9f898d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265543} {:answer/id #uuid "98e7f342-3eca-55c4-886d-a453ee824c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc4900d-2a15-5533-bc48-6fc1b9f898d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265543} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b583a18-f6cb-53fa-b23a-abf13a9c85c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9cf73f95-3551-5a58-91c6-cbb7c7b5cef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72843" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$"}]}
